.TH FKSTRING 3 2026-09-24 "fkstring" "fkstring Library Manual"
.nh
.SH NAME
fkstring, fkstrlen, fkstrsize, fkcstr, FKSTR_NPOS \- length-tracked, growable C strings
.SH LIBRARY
fkstring library
.RI ( libfkstring ,\~ \-lfkstring )
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <fkstring.h>
.PP
.B typedef struct _fkstring {
.BR "    size_t  len;" "      /* bytes of content, excluding the trailing NUL */"
.BR "    size_t  alloc;" "    /* allocated size of cstr, in bytes */"
.BR "    char   *cstr;" "     /* NUL-terminated buffer; NULL when len == 0 */"
.B } fkstring;
.PP
.BI "size_t fkstrlen(const fkstring *" fks );
.BI "size_t fkstrsize(const fkstring *" fks );
.BI "char *fkcstr(const fkstring *" fks );
.PP
.B #define FKSTR_NPOS ((size_t)\-1)
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
An
.B fkstring
is a heap-allocated string that records its own length
.RI ( len )
and buffer size
.RI ( alloc ),
so that operations never need to scan for a terminating NUL byte
and appends do not reallocate on every call.
.PP
.BR fkstrlen (),
.BR fkstrsize (),
and
.BR fkcstr ()
are macros that yield the
.IR len ,
.IR alloc ,
and
.I cstr
fields of
.IR fks .
They evaluate
.I fks
once and do not check it for NULL.
.PP
.B FKSTR_NPOS
is the offset returned by the search functions when there is no match.
.SS Invariants
Every
.B fkstring
created or modified by the library satisfies the following:
.IP \(bu 3
If
.I len
is 0, then
.I alloc
is 0 and
.I cstr
is NULL.
An empty
.B fkstring
owns no buffer.
Code that passes
.I cstr
to a libc function must allow for this, for example with
.IR "fkstrlen(s) ? fkcstr(s) : \(dq\(dq" .
.IP \(bu
Otherwise
.I len
is less than
.IR alloc ,
and
.IR cstr [ len ]
is a NUL byte, so
.I cstr
can be passed to functions expecting a C string.
.IP \(bu
.I len
is authoritative, not the NUL terminator.
A string may contain embedded NUL bytes (see
.BR fkstrnewb (3)),
and all library functions honor them.
A C-string function applied to
.I cstr
stops at the first embedded NUL.
.PP
Programs may read the fields directly, and may modify the bytes in
.IR cstr [0]
through
.IR cstr [ len "\-1]."
Changing the fields themselves, or freeing
.IR cstr ,
is undefined behavior.
Every
.B fkstring
must eventually be released with
.BR fkstrdestroy (3).
.SS Memory growth
Buffers are sized with hysteresis.
Growth allocates 143% of the needed length (at least 16 bytes),
so a run of appends reallocates only a logarithmic number of times.
Shrinking (by
.BR fkstrtrunc (3)
and the functions built on it) returns memory only once the buffer
exceeds 350% of the new length and is larger than 16 bytes,
so strings that repeatedly grow and shrink do not reallocate on each call.
.BR fkslack (3)
and
.BR fkfit (3)
set the buffer size explicitly, bypassing these thresholds.
.PP
These parameters are global variables, declared in
.IR <fkstring_internal.h> :
.TP
.BR "int _bumpfactor" " (default 143)"
Growth factor, in percent.
.TQ
.BR "int _deflatefactor" " (default 350)"
Shrink threshold, in percent of the new length.
.TQ
.BR "size_t _minalloc" " (default 16)"
Minimum buffer size.
.TQ
.BR "size_t _sprintftry" " (default 48)"
First-guess buffer size for formatting into an empty string; see
.BR fksprintf (3).
.TQ
.BR "size_t _slurptry" " (default 4096)"
Initial buffer size when
.BR fkslurp (3)
reads a descriptor with no size hint.
.TQ
.BR "size_t _catbufsize" " (default 65536)"
Copy buffer size for
.BR fkcat (3).
.PP
Assigning to them affects every
.B fkstring
in the process.
.I _bumpfactor
must be at least 100, and
.IR _sprintftry ,
.IR _slurptry ,
and
.I _catbufsize
must be nonzero.
.SS Error handling
Functions that return a pointer return NULL only for invalid arguments,
such as a NULL
.BR "fkstring *" ,
and for I/O errors where noted.
Memory allocation never fails from the caller's point of view: when
.BR malloc (3)
or
.BR realloc (3)
fails, the library prints a message to standard error and terminates the
process (see
.BR DIAGNOSTICS ).
A length computation that would overflow
.B size_t
is handled the same way.
