.SH DIAGNOSTICS
On an unrecoverable error the library writes one of the following
messages to standard error and calls
.BR exit (3)
with status 253:
.TP
.B Memory alloc error
.BR malloc (3),
.BR calloc (3),
or
.BR realloc (3)
failed.
.TP
.B Length arithmetic overflow
A resulting length would not fit in a
.BR size_t .
.TP
.B Inconsistency in vsnprintf() behavior
.BR vsnprintf (3)
reported a length on the first pass that did not fit on the second; see
.BR fksprintf (3).
Formatting errors themselves are returned to the caller instead.
.SH ATTRIBUTES
Functions operating on distinct
.B fkstring
objects may be called concurrently from different threads.
Concurrent access to the same
.BR fkstring ,
where at least one access modifies it, needs external locking.
The tuning variables are not protected; change them, if at all,
before starting other threads.
.SH NOTES
.I <fkstring_internal.h>
is installed alongside
.I <fkstring.h>
for the tuning variables above.
Its other contents (the error codes and the inline helpers
.BR fkpanic (),
.BR fkaddlen (),
.BR fkmullen (),
and
.BR allocforlen ())
are internal to the library and may change.
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
#include <stdio.h>
#include <fkstring.h>
\&
int
main(void)
{
    fkstring  *s, **words;
\&
    s = fkstrnew("  hello, world  ");
    fktrim(s);
    fkstrcatf(s, " (%zu bytes)", fkstrlen(s));
    puts(fkcstr(s));             /* hello, world (12 bytes) */
\&
    words = fksplit(s, \(aq \(aq);
    printf("%s\en", fkcstr(words[1]));   /* world */
\&
    fkarraydestroy(words);
    fkstrdestroy(s);
    return 0;
}
.EE
